/*
Problem: Find Target Indices After Sorting Array (LC 2089)
Topic: Array / Sorting

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Sort the array first.

After sorting, every occurrence of the
target will appear consecutively.

Traverse the sorted array and store
all indices where the value equals
the target.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Sort the array.

2. Initialize an empty answer vector.

3. Traverse the sorted array.

4. If:

   nums[i] == target

   store index i.

5. Return the answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

nums = [1,2,5,2,3]

target = 2

Sorted:

[1,2,2,3,5]

Target indices:

1

2

Answer:

[1,2]

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

nums = [1,2,5,2,3]

target = 4

Sorted:

[1,2,2,3,5]

4 is absent.

Answer:

[]

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Sorting places equal elements next to
each other.

A single traversal after sorting
collects every index where the target
appears.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n log n)

Sorting dominates.

Traversal is O(n).

Space : O(1)

Ignoring the output vector.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Sorting + Array Traversal
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> targetIndices(vector<int>& nums,
                              int target) {

        // Sort the array
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        vector<int> ans;

        // Collect all target indices
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {

            if (nums[i] == target)
                ans.push_back(i);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
